{
  "extends": "./tsconfig.json",
  // The JavaScript the build runs, checked as JavaScript. This is deliberately
  // NARROWER than `scripts/`, and the narrowness is the decision (#183):
  //
  //   `scripts/feature/`  is here. It is the best-annotated of the three bodies
  //                       under `scripts/`, and its failures are the most
  //                       expensive — `land` runs its teardown AFTER the work is
  //                       already on `development`, so a throw there exits 1 on
  //                       a change that has landed. #170 shipped exactly that.
  //   `astro.config.mjs`  is here because it was the other file `include` was
  //                       claiming to cover and did not.
  //   `scripts/checks/`, `scripts/editor/` are NOT here, and are in no project
  //                       at all rather than in one that reads as covering them.
  //                       `include: ["scripts/**/*"]` here is 1351 errors on
  //                       2026-08-29 — 860 of them `scripts/editor/`, 436
  //                       `scripts/checks/` — and that residue is the whole
  //                       reason this is narrow. Widening it is this `include`
  //                       line and their own residue; there is nothing else to
  //                       undo.
  //   `design/`           is NOT here either, and nothing else claims it — its
  //                       generators are run by hand rather than by the build,
  //                       so a type error in one costs a run and not a deploy.
  //                       That holds even for `design/eater-slab/retheme.mjs`,
  //                       which the Agent Contract names as a seam: its
  //                       `@typedef`s are documentation, and `pnpm test` is what
  //                       actually covers it. Said here because #183's rule is
  //                       that a file is in a project or in none, honestly.
  //   `scripts/static-tree.mjs` is here because `scripts/feature/` imports it,
  //                       so a program built from that `include` checks it
  //                       whether it is named or not. Named anyway, WITH its
  //                       test: leaving it to the import would check the module
  //                       and not the test beside it, and "which files does this
  //                       cover" should be answerable by reading this line.
  "include": [
    "scripts/feature/**/*",
    "scripts/static-tree.mjs",
    "scripts/static-tree.test.mjs",
    "astro.config.mjs"
  ],
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "types": ["node"]
  }
}
